hersburg, MD, United States"}},"id":"8928cf10f74246839514ab74c54448b5"},{"sequence":4,"submissionId":20080012612,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Yang, Jiann"},"organization":{"name":"National Inst. of Standards and Technology","location":"Gaithersburg, MD, United States"}},"id":"e1d034a319704290b4007452339d089a"},{"sequence":5,"submissionId":20080012612,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Mulholland, George"},"organization":{"name":"Maryland Univ.","location":"College Park, MD, United States"}},"id":"bd46ebcce5f14416b43bc1829705eb3b"},{"sequence":6,"submissionId":20080012612,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Yuan, Zeng-guang"},"organization":{"name":"National Center for Space Exploration Research on Fluids and Combustion","location":"Cleveland, OH, United States"}},"id":"e69e5caba10746e8acfb535cd4a25b2e"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20080012612,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-08-24T05:09:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Results from a recent smoke particle size measurement experiment conducted on the International Space Station (ISS) are presented along with the results from a model of the transport of smoke in the ISS. The experimental results show that, for the materials tested, a substantial portion of the smoke particles are below 500 nm in diameter. The smoke transport model demonstrated that mixing dominates the smoke transport and that consequently detection times are longer than in normal gravity.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"country":"United States","submissionId":20080012612,"endDate":"2008-01-10T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","name":"2008 AIAA Aerospace Sciences Meeting","location":"Reno, NV","id":"0c7e2feddc0f43339581fbb0e5bf5785","startDate":"2008-01-07T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20080012612,"id":"c11e752b7b134c708c09a93027785743","publicationDate":"2007-01-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"20080012612.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20080012612/downloads/20080012612.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20080012612/downloads/20080012612.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20080012612/downloads/20080012612.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":294.94836},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"GOV_PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20170007283,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"5a629f42db984b09a6fe1b25d2e2775f","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Engineering (General)"],"keywords":["Aerosol","Spacecraft Fire Safety","Smoke"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20170007283,"ear":"NO","id":"46cf89a157c544c5872e0f47b24d9520","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["GRC-E-DAA-TN32421","Report Number: GRC-E-DAA-TN32421"],"fundingNumbers":[{"number":"WBS 067463.01.99.99.99.99.22","submissionId":20170007283,"id":"3a4f193f187245029137f66d392b9c95","type":"WBS"}],"title":"Spacecraft Fire Safety Research at NASA Glenn Research Center","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PAPER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2017-08-03T13:59:26.3630000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20170007283,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Meyer, Marit"},"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH United States"}},"id":"349b3027b713410caff049182f866a53"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20170007283,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2017-08-03T13:59:26.3630000+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Appropriate design of fire detection systems requires knowledge of both the expected fire signature and the background aerosol levels. Terrestrial fire detection systems have been developed based on extensive study of terrestrial fires. Unfortunately there is no corresponding data set for spacecraft fires and consequently the fire detectors in current spacecraft were developed based upon terrestrial designs. In low gravity, buoyant flow is negligible which causes particles to concentrate at the smoke source, increasing their residence time, and increasing the transport time to smoke detectors. Microgravity fires have significantly different structure than those in 1-g which can change the formation history of the smoke particles. Finally the materials used in spacecraft are different from typical terrestrial environments where smoke properties have been evaluated. It is critically important to detect a fire in its early phase before a flame is established, given the fixed volume of air on any spacecraft. Consequently, the primary target for spacecraft fire detection is pyrolysis products rather than soot. Experimental investigations have been performed at three different NASA facilities which characterize smoke aerosols from overheating common spacecraft materials. The earliest effort consists of aerosol measurements in low gravity, called the Smoke Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME), and subsequent ground-based testing of SAME smoke in 55-gallon drums with an aerosol reference instrument. Another set of experiments were performed at NASAs Johnson Space Center White Sands Test Facility (WSTF), with additional fuels and an alternate smoke production method. Measurements of these smoke products include mass and number concentration, and a thermal precipitator was designed for this investigation to capture particles for microscopic analysis. The final experiments presented are from NASAs Gases and Aerosols from Smoldering Polymers (GASP) Laboratory, with selected results focusing on realistic fuel preparations and heating profiles with regards to early detection of smoke. SAFFIRE is the upcoming large-scale fire experiment which will be executed in a Cygnus vehicle after it undocks from the ISS. ","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"country":"Israel","submissionId":20170007283,"endDate":"2016-09-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"organizationId":"8bf72e8022f6474c8908c31bc079ddaa","meta":{"organization":{"name":"Technion - Israel Inst. of Tech.","location":"Haifa, Israel"}},"meetingId":"1527c46019234dc8a990e076af5002a5","id":"67aa466b18884d49b7df91baf56fe352"}],"name":"Space Studies Program (SSP 16)","location":"Haifa","id":"1527c46019234dc8a990e076af5002a5","startDate":"2016-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20170007283,"id":"a2009826a08c4e0ca9c141bc6f3e49cc","publicationDate":"2016-08-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"20170007283.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20170007283/downloads/20170007283.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20170007283/downloads/20170007283.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20170007283/downloads/20170007283.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":228.9088},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"licenseType":"NO","submissionId":20230013826,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"200082db8aee4c2aa1cea9292af870ff","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Lunar and Planetary Science and Exploration"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20230013826,"ear":"NO","id":"e442c252f70b495ba94f22adba6e84ab","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2023-10-31T04:00:00.0000000+00:00","fundingNumbers":[{"number":"089407.02.04.22","submissionId":20230013826,"id":"7bbc601123524f11a7b44b1c7be9a056","type":"WBS"}],"title":"The Influence of Smoke Particle Properties and Cabin Characteristics on Smoke Detection in Lunar Gravity","stiType":"POSTER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2023-09-25T18:29:23.4016650+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"organizationId":"0be91b7a68665cf882c2ccee8ad03aff","sequence":0,"submissionId":20230013826,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Claire Fortenberry"},"organization":{"name":"Universities Space Research Association","location":"Columbia, Maryland, United States"}},"id":"2fa55af3889a49f0a71d292d232d0c0c","userType":"CIVIL","userId":"b9cd289657ad4b388c09d1692901b134"},{"organizationId":"ef57c2628ac159568c05f9b33526619e","sequence":1,"submissionId":20230013826,"meta":{"author":{"orcidId":"","name":"Ramaswamy Balasubramaniam"},"organization":{"name":"Case Western Reserve University","location":"Cleveland, Ohio, United States"}},"id":"7a5bee7ee6dc4bf89ce82cdcaf7c8663","userType":"CONTRACTOR_GRANTEE","userId":"ebbe0da160ad4d19996d65d3a7e96e13"},{"organizationId":"fdea3daf3b5156699f511edf3e9b9ec4","sequence":2,"submissionId":20230013826,"meta":{"author":{"orcidId":"","name":"David Urban"},"organization":{"name":"Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, Ohio, United States"}},"id":"2103a2c00b8a4baaac5cb8740c6b91af","userType":"CIVIL","userId":"e6ad9f3a70a94fc1a0a4a56bb211746a"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Poster","technicalReviewType":"SINGLE_EXPERT","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20230013826,"created":"2023-09-25T03:12:41.6779780+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Spacecraft fires pose a threat to the success of future Lunar, Martian, and deep space exploration missions. As NASA plans to return humans to the Moon in the next decade, novel mission requirements will present new fire safety challenges. For example, materials that are fire resistant on Earth are expected to burn under planned habitat conditions (elevated oxygen concentrations and reduced cabin pressure) and partial gravity (0.16g). Optimal smoke detector placement will depend on a combination of buoyant plume velocities, induced by partial gravity, and Environmental Control and Life Support Systems (ECLSS) parameters, including particle filtration rates, supply and return placement within the cabin, and forced air velocities. These ECLSS parameters must also address the need for rapid Lunar dust removal, as Lunar dust exposure poses a risk to crew health and hardware functionality.\n\nHere, we present progress toward a computational fluid dynamics model to evaluate smoke transport in a Lunar habitat. Recent work has demonstrated that if air supplies are placed on ceilings and returns on the floor, a buoyant smoke layer at the ceiling may disperse over the order of minutes even under low forced flow conditions. We expand upon these results to examine the influence of different supply and return configurations on smoke plume development. Additionally, we also address differences in the transport of smoke particles and Lunar dust by varying particle parameters like size, density, and shape factor. Finally, we discuss ongoing and future experimental efforts to measure smoke particle properties and transport under partial gravity, elevated oxygen, and reduced pressure conditions.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","cui":{"isCui":false,"submissionId":20230013826,"id":"1a5c6fbc41b84d19a3d0a16cd58d3a28"},"meetings":[{"country":"US","submissionId":20230013826,"endDate":"2023-10-06T04:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"organizationId":"497c8a33c13c549fac158b25ce5dee10","meta":{"organization":{"name":"American Association for Aerosol Research","location":"Reston, Virginia, United States"}},"meetingId":"1520362645774d1caafc4bece1201dff","id":"a3d644128c6242f6bf7c61d1f440fb56"}],"name":"American Association for Aerosol Research 41st Annual Conference","location":"Portland, OR","id":"1520362645774d1caafc4bece1201dff","url":"https://www.aaar.org/2023/","startDate":"2023-10-02T04:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"AAAR 2023 v1.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20230013826/downloads/AAAR%202023%20v1.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20230013826/downloads/AAAR%202023%20v1.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20230013826/downloads/AAAR%202023%20v1.pdf.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"}],"aggregations":{"created":{"buckets":[{"key_as_string":"2013","key":1356998400000,"doc_count":6},{"key_as_string":"2017","key":1483228800000,"doc_count":1},{"key_as_string":"2023","key":1672531200000,"doc_count":1}]},"author":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"Nahra, Henry","doc_count":2},{"key":"Sacksteder, Kurt","doc_count":2},{"key":"Urban, David L.","doc_count":2},{"key":"Brooker, John E.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Claire Fortenberry","doc_count":1},{"key":"Cleary, Thomas","doc_count":1},{"key":"David Urban","doc_count":1},{"key":"Gard, Melissa Y.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Griffin, DeVon W.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Meyer, Marit","doc_count":1},{"key":"Mulholland, George","doc_count":1},{"key":"Ramaswamy Balasubramaniam","doc_co